  "preamble": "Smoothing of the diphthong [ai] to a monophthong [a]/[aː] by loss of the front glide, often with compensatory length. In Old English, Proto-Germanic *ai regularly became ā (*stainaz > stān 'stone'), while Old Frisian and Old Saxon raised it to ē instead. In Southern American English the PRICE vowel /aɪ/ loses its glide to [aː], especially before voiced consonants and word-finally (Stage 1 of the Southern Vowel Shift). In Brazilian Portuguese /aj/ is realized as [a] before the postalveolar fricatives /ʃ, ʒ/ (baixo [ˈbaʃu], caixa [ˈkaʃɐ]). Georgian lost the post-vocalic glide written ჲ, so Old Georgian final -ay became -a (დედაჲ deday > დედა deda 'mother').",
  "phoneticEffects": "Monophthongization, Glide Deletion, Compensatory Lengthening",
